## What was happening

When a query fails in `clickhouse-local`, the exception can be set on `state->exception` during `sendQuery` (line ~366). On the subsequent `poll()` call, the first exception block (line 458) immediately set `next_packet_type = Protocol::Server::Exception` and returned — **skipping the buffered log flush entirely**. The client would then consume the exception packet and stop, so any `Trace`/`Debug` log messages accumulated before the failure were discarded.

The second exception block later in `poll()` (line ~502, after the execution loop) already guarded against this by calling `needSendLogs()` first, but the early-return path did not.

## The fix

In `src/Client/LocalConnection.cpp`, I added a `needSendLogs()` check to the first exception block so it matches the existing pattern:

```cpp
    if (state->exception)
    {
        if (needSendLogs())
            return true;

        next_packet_type = Protocol::Server::Exception;
        return true;
    }
```

Now when an exception is pending, `poll` first drains any buffered log messages. `needSendLogs` pops the queued rows, sets `next_packet_type = Protocol::Server::Log`, and returns `true` — so the logs are delivered to the client first. Only once the log queue is empty does `poll` fall through to set the `Exception` packet. This ensures the `Trace`/`Debug` lines (including the `UNKNOWN_TABLE` context) reach stderr before the exception is reported.

I changed only the source file and left `tests/` untouched per your instructions.

